{{smaller block|Sophy, love, take your guitar, and ''thrum'' in with the boy a little.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xvii.}}}}

2. To drum or tap idly on something with the fingers.

{{smaller block|I'll not stand all day ''thrumming'',<br>But quickly shoot my bolt.
{{right|''Middleton'', Women Beware Women, iii. 3.}}
I sit, my empty glass reversed,<br>And ''thrumming'' on the table.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Will Waterproof.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' 1. To play idly or unskilfully on (some stringed instrument) with the fingers; sound by fingering in a listless or monotonous manner.—2. To drum or tap idly on.

{{smaller block|For late, when bees to change their chimes began,<br>How did I see them ''thrum'' the frying-pan!
{{right|''Shenstone'', Colemira, st. 7.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To thrum over''', to tell over in a monotonous manner.}}

'''thrum'''<sup>2</sup> (thrum), ''n.'' [< ''thrum''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.''] A monotonous sound, as from the careless or unskilful fingering of a guitar or harp.

{{smaller block|As I drew near I heard the tinkle of a triangle and the ''thrum'' of a harp accompanying a weird chant.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXVII. 253.}}}}

'''thrum'''<sup>3</sup>†, ''n.'' [ME., also ''throm'', *''thrym'', < AS. ''thrymm'', power, glory.] 1. A troop.—2. A heap.

'''thrumble'''† (thrum′bl), ''v.'' [< ME. ''thrumblen'', ''thromlen'', ''thrompelen'', stumble.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To stumble.

{{smaller block|He ''thromlede'' [var. ''thrumbled''] at the threshefold.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), vii. 408.}}}}

